titute of Political Studies, was established in 1872 in Paris, France, as a response to the political turmoil in Europe following the Franco-Prussian War. It emerged as a pivotal institution for higher education, particularly in social sciences and political studies, attracting influential figures such as alumni and faculty members like former French President François Hollande and political theorist Pierre Rosanvallon. Its significance lies in its role as a breeding ground for political leadership and civil service in France and beyond, shaping the discourse around governance, public policy, and international relations.

